From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 02:55:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Run chunk decoration on its own thread

Decoration - ores, trees, plants - is three quarters of what populating a chunk
costs and touches neither entities nor tile entities, so it is the piece that
can leave the server thread. It runs on one thread for the whole server, which
keeps it exactly as serial as it has always been: BiomeDecorator, WorldGenBigTree
and the flower generators all keep state on the biome singleton while they run,
so decorating two chunks at once would produce mangled trees, not faster ones.

Its world calls go into a sandbox. Reads come from an overlay over the chunks
captured when the job was made; writes go into an ordered log the server thread
replays through the real setTypeAndData, so lighting, client updates and physics
still happen there. The height map and precipitation height are derived from the
overlay rather than mirrored, because maintaining them the way Chunk does would
mean calling back into the World through onPlace and skylight propagation.

WorldGenLiquids does not merely place blocks - it drives the liquid's own update
logic through forceBlockUpdateTick, which flips a field on the World and reaches
into the scheduled tick list. That call is recorded and replayed on the server
thread instead, with real physics, after the rest of the chunk's decoration.

Anything the sandbox cannot model marks the job and the server thread decorates
inline, from the same point in the random sequence - which is what SnapshotRandom
is for. No escape occurred in any test run.

Terrain is no longer bit-identical, which the fork now allows for generation: a
chunk's decoration sees the world as of when its job was made rather than as of
the moment it runs. Every feature is still there at its usual density - over 625
chunks, ores within 2%, logs and leaves +5%, and nothing missing. The two visible
shifts are both from the deferred liquid update: obsidian 544 -> 796 and flowing
water 130 -> 26, against stationary water and lava within 1%.

Measured on a walk: decoration on the server thread fell from 22.8% to nothing
for the overworld, against 9.4% spent replaying the writes. Pre-generating a
world is not faster - there the server thread has nothing else to do with the
time - so the gain is in live play, where it does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BiomeBase.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BiomeBase.java
index edfef87cd9e0629899bfd8e7592600a20455a2c9..1d5172b2693eb4f61588eb00e76b9eeac32eadef 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BiomeBase.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/BiomeBase.java
@@ -74,7 +74,22 @@ public abstract class BiomeBase {
     public static final BiomeBase ad = BiomeBase.OCEAN;
     protected static final NoiseGenerator3 temperatureNoise;
     protected static final NoiseGenerator3 GRASS_COLOR_NOISE;
-    protected static final WorldGenTallPlant DOUBLE_PLANT_GENERATOR;
+    // Athena start - one double-plant generator per thread.
+    //
+    // It was a single static instance, and generating one is two steps: set the plant type, then
+    // place it. With the overworld decorating on its own thread while another world decorates on the
+    // server thread, the two can interleave between those steps and put down the wrong flower.
+    private static final ThreadLocal<WorldGenTallPlant> DOUBLE_PLANT_GENERATORS = new ThreadLocal<WorldGenTallPlant>() {
+        @Override
+        protected WorldGenTallPlant initialValue() {
+            return new WorldGenTallPlant();
+        }
+    };
+
+    protected static WorldGenTallPlant doublePlantGenerator() {
+        return BiomeBase.DOUBLE_PLANT_GENERATORS.get();
+    }
+    // Athena end
     public String biomeName;
     public int color;
     public int aj;
@@ -471,7 +486,6 @@ public abstract class BiomeBase {
         BiomeBase.explorationBiomesList.remove(BiomeBase.SMALL_MOUNTAINS);
         temperatureNoise = new NoiseGenerator3(new Random(1234L), 1);
         GRASS_COLOR_NOISE = new NoiseGenerator3(new Random(2345L), 1);
-        DOUBLE_PLANT_GENERATOR = new WorldGenTallPlant();
     }
 
     static class SyntheticClass_1 {
